1. The Problem Statement (PS)
Core problem statement: Develop a secure, centralized, scalable and user-friendly Security Deposit/Fixed Deposit Receipt (FDR) Management Portal for the All India Council for Technical Education (AICTE) that streamlines the complete lifecycle of security deposits and FDRs associated with applicable institutional approval and compliance processes.

The platform must enable AICTE-approved institutions to submit financial instrument details and supporting documents, allow authorized officials to review and verify those records, automatically monitor maturity and renewal deadlines, track replacement instruments, manage release or refund requests through authorized approval workflows, and generate real-time dashboards and regulatory reports.

The system should improve record accuracy, reduce repetitive manual work, minimize avoidable administrative delays and provide transparent, auditable oversight of financial instruments.

The central objective is to establish one reliable source of information for every deposit and FDR, from initial submission through verification, renewal and eventual release.

The solution should combine conventional software engineering, automation and appropriate AI technologies while maintaining strict security, privacy, regulatory compliance and human oversight of financial decisions.

The scope includes:
- Centralized institution-wise and instrument-wise records.
- Secure document submission, storage and retrieval.
- Automated validation and discrepancy detection.
- OCR-based extraction of information from FDR documents.
- Validity and maturity monitoring with configurable notifications.
- Renewal, replacement and release/refund workflows.
- Dashboards, reports, access control and audit trails.
- Integration capability for existing AICTE systems and authorized bank-verification services.
The prototype must demonstrate the end-to-end workflow using realistic test scenarios. Where actual AICTE or bank integrations are unavailable, simulated data and verification results must be clearly identified as such.

2. Comprehensive Research Summary
2.1 Background and core operational challenge
AICTE regulates technical institutions through applicable approval requirements, regulations and administrative procedures. Depending on the relevant requirements, institutions may need to submit or maintain security deposits or FDRs.

Managing these instruments involves more than maintaining a list of documents. Each instrument has financial details, validity dates, verification requirements, supporting records and administrative actions associated with it.

The key challenge is maintaining accurate, current and traceable information across the complete lifecycle.

For example, when an institution submits an FDR, officials need to establish what the institution has declared, what information appears on the document, whether the instrument has been independently verified, when action is required and whether the relevant regulatory conditions have been fulfilled.

If an instrument is renewed, the system must connect the replacement with the previous instrument. If a release request is submitted, it must track the request and preserve the authorization history.

A fragmented process can create duplicated records, inconsistent information, missed deadlines, delayed reviews and limited visibility into pending cases.
2.2 Functional requirements identified
The requirements in the problem statement can be organized into eight functional areas.
Functional area	Required capability
Record management	Maintain institution-wise and instrument-wise records of FDRs and security deposits.
Document management	Upload, store, retrieve and review FDRs and supporting documents securely.
Verification	Record verification evidence, identify inconsistencies and distinguish submitted information from independently confirmed information.
Validity monitoring	Track issue dates, maturity dates, validity status and applicable deadlines.
Renewal management	Track renewed or replacement instruments and retain historical relationships.
Release/refund workflow	Submit, review and track requests through authorized approval procedures.
Alerts and reporting	Generate reminders, escalate unresolved cases and produce dashboards and regulatory reports.
Security and interoperability	Enforce access controls, encryption, audit trails and authorized integration with existing systems.

These functions form the baseline against which the proposed solution should be evaluated.
2.3 What the system must distinguish
One of the most important architectural findings is that the system should not treat all information as equally trustworthy.
Submitted information

Details entered by the institution, including the declared FDR number, amount and dates.



Extracted information

Fields read from the uploaded document by OCR or document parsing. These may contain extraction errors and must be checked.



Verified information

Information confirmed through an authorized bank or financial source, or reviewed through a documented verification process. The verification method and evidence should be recorded.



Approved action

A formal decision made by an authorized official under the applicable procedure. Verification does not itself imply approval for a release or refund.



This distinction is fundamental to preventing the portal from accidentally presenting unverified or AI-extracted information as an established financial fact.
2.4 AI feasibility assessment
Overall assessment: Feasible, with AI supporting selected tasks rather than replacing the complete regulatory workflow.

The project does not require a custom AI model to be useful. Existing OCR tools, standard Python validation logic, database constraints, scheduled tasks and established authentication mechanisms can deliver most of the required functionality.

AI is most valuable where information must be extracted from variable document layouts, unusual patterns need to be highlighted, or lengthy case documents need to be summarized.
Capability	Recommended approach	Feasibility
Read FDR documents	OCR and document parsing	High, subject to document quality
Compare extracted and entered values	Deterministic validation rules	High
Detect duplicate references	Database constraints and matching rules	High
Detect unusual records	Rules initially; anomaly detection if data supports it	Moderate to high
Monitor maturity dates	Date calculations and scheduled jobs	High
Send reminders and escalations	Notification service and workflow rules	High
Summarize cases or approved policies	Existing language model, optionally with retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)	Feasible, with factuality controls
Verify FDR authenticity	Authorized bank-verification mechanisms	Dependent on access and integration
Approve financial releases	Authorized workflow and human decision	Must follow applicable procedures

A. Computer vision and OCR
OCR can convert scanned or image-based FDRs into machine-readable text. Document parsing can then identify fields such as the issuing bank, instrument reference, deposit amount, issue date and maturity date.

Potential tools include:
- Tesseract OCR.
- PaddleOCR.
- PyMuPDF or pdfplumber for PDFs that contain extractable text.
- A pretrained document-understanding model if simpler extraction methods do not perform adequately.
The system should retain field-level confidence information and, where possible, the source location in the document. Low-confidence fields should be sent for human review.
B. Rule-based validation and anomaly detection
The first validation layer should use explicit rules:
- Required fields are present.
- Dates are valid and logically consistent.
- Extracted amounts match the declared amounts.
- Reference numbers are not duplicated where uniqueness is expected.
- Renewal records link to the correct previous instrument.
- Required actions are not overdue.
- Records that conflict with applicable business rules are flagged.
An anomaly-detection model, such as Isolation Forest, could later identify unusual patterns in suitable numerical or categorical features. It should not be introduced without representative data and evaluation.

An anomaly score is an indication for investigation, not proof of fraud or invalidity.
C. Generative AI
A language model can help officials understand lengthy case histories, summarize discrepancies, draft reminder messages and answer questions about approved policy documents.

If a policy assistant is included, retrieval-augmented generation should retrieve relevant, authorized source documents and present citations. Regulatory decisions should remain based on the actual applicable requirements, not unsupported model-generated interpretations.
D. Predictive analytics
Predicting which cases are likely to become overdue is a possible future enhancement. It would require historical records of submissions, deadlines, follow-ups and outcomes.

For the initial prototype, explicit deadline rules are more reliable, transparent and easier to demonstrate than a predictive model.
2.5 Data requirements and sources
The system requires both structured records and unstructured documents.
Data category	Examples	Purpose
Institution data	Institution identifier, name, approval reference and contact details	Identify the responsible institution
Instrument data	FDR reference, bank, amount, issue date and maturity date	Track the financial instrument
Document data	Uploaded PDF/image, file metadata and checksum	Preserve supporting evidence
Extraction data	OCR fields, confidence scores and corrections	Support automated document processing
Verification data	Source, result, timestamp, reference and reviewer	Record verification evidence
Workflow data	Renewal requests, release requests, observations and decisions	Track administrative progress
Notification data	Recipient, deadline, delivery status and retry history	Demonstrate reminder reliability
Audit data	Actor, action, timestamp and change history	Maintain accountability

For a hackathon, the dataset can consist of synthetic institutions and synthetic FDR documents with deliberately introduced errors. These could include mismatched amounts, missing fields, duplicated references, invalid dates, approaching maturity and replacement instruments.

For a production system, actual records, regulatory rules and verification data would require authorized access and appropriate data-governance arrangements.
2.6 Recommended technology stack
The stack should be straightforward enough to implement rapidly while supporting a future production architecture.
Layer	Recommended technology
Frontend	HTML, CSS and JavaScript; React if useful
Backend	Django and Django REST Framework
Database	MySQL or PostgreSQL
Document storage	Private file storage for the prototype; private object storage for production
OCR	Tesseract or PaddleOCR
PDF processing	PyMuPDF or pdfplumber
Validation	Python rules and database constraints
Anomaly detection	scikit-learn, if justified by data
Background jobs	Celery and Redis, or a simpler scheduler for a small demo
Dashboard	Chart.js or equivalent
Authentication	Django authentication and role-based access control
Notifications	Email and in-app notification services
Optional AI assistant	Existing LLM with controlled retrieval over approved documents

A modular Django application is an appropriate starting point. It allows the team to deliver the core system first and add AI capabilities incrementally.
2.7 Security, privacy and governance
The platform handles confidential institutional and financial information, so security must be incorporated from the beginning.

The proposed controls include:
- Role-based permissions for institutions, reviewers and administrators.
- Secure authentication and appropriate session management.
- Encryption in transit and at rest.
- Restricted document downloads and private storage.
- File-type validation, upload-size limits and malware scanning.
- Audit logs for submissions, edits, verification results and approvals.
- Backup, recovery and retention procedures.
- Configurable regulatory rules with controlled updates.
- Human review of uncertain OCR outputs and suspicious records.
- Clearly identified simulated results during prototype demonstrations.
The system should not automatically release funds, declare a document genuine from its appearance alone, or treat a missing external verification response as proof of fraud.
2.8 Evaluation and success criteria
The problem statement expects measurable improvements, not merely a working interface.

The evaluation should establish a baseline and compare manual processing with the proposed workflow.
Metric	Evaluation method
OCR field accuracy	Compare extracted fields with manually verified ground truth
Discrepancy detection precision	Measure how many flagged issues are genuine issues
Discrepancy detection recall	Measure how many known issues the system catches
Deadline monitoring reliability	Check that all configured reminders are generated correctly
Duplicate detection	Test known duplicates and legitimate distinct instruments
Processing time	Compare manual and automated processing times
Workflow turnaround	Measure time spent in submission, review and approval stages
Audit completeness	Verify that important actions and decisions are traceable

No numerical improvement should be claimed until the prototype has been tested against a defined dataset and baseline.
